nono 0.80 for musl: classify an open's access from the kernel's two access-mode bits.

The seccomp supervisor classifies a trapped open by `flags & libc::O_ACCMODE`. On musl,
O_ACCMODE is `03|O_PATH`, so every `O_PATH` open (`O_RDONLY|O_PATH`: systemd's `/proc/` and
`/proc/1/root` probes, fd-based path walks) comes out as neither read-only nor write-only and is
treated as read-write: the static build asks to "Read and write" such paths and denies them,
without asking, even inside read-only grants such as `/nix/store`. The glibc build (O_ACCMODE is
3) reports them as reads. Masking with 0o3, the kernel's own access mode, gives the same answer
on both.

--- a/crates/nono/src/sandbox/linux.rs
+++ b/crates/nono/src/sandbox/linux.rs
@@ -1665,11 +1665,12 @@
 
 /// Classify access mode from open flags.
 ///
-/// Extracts O_ACCMODE bits and maps to AccessMode. Used by both openat (where flags
+/// Extracts the access-mode bits and maps to AccessMode. Used by both openat (where flags
 /// come from args[2] directly) and openat2 (where flags come from open_how.flags).
+/// The kernel's access mode is the low two bits; musl's O_ACCMODE also includes O_PATH.
 #[must_use]
 pub fn classify_access_from_flags(flags: i32) -> crate::AccessMode {
-    match flags & libc::O_ACCMODE {
+    match flags & 0o3 {
         libc::O_RDONLY => crate::AccessMode::Read,
         libc::O_WRONLY => crate::AccessMode::Write,
         _ => crate::AccessMode::ReadWrite,
